1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,020
Can you show me troubleshooting Kubernetes?

2
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:15,829
Troubleshooting is finding out why the cluster does not do what you asked, with a method instead of guesses.

3
00:00:15,829 --> 00:00:19,730
Kubernetes is unusually good at telling you what is wrong:

4
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:24,034
every object has a status, every important step leaves an event,

5
00:00:24,034 --> 00:00:28,741
every container keeps its logs, and you can step inside a running Pod.

6
00:00:28,741 --> 00:00:32,103
The skill is knowing which of these to read first.

7
00:00:32,103 --> 00:00:34,592
A doctor does not start with surgery:

8
00:00:34,592 --> 00:00:40,711
they ask what hurts, take your temperature, look at the test results, and only then decide.

9
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:43,009
Something always breaks:

10
00:00:43,009 --> 00:00:46,784
a typo in a label, an image tag that does not exist,

11
00:00:46,784 --> 00:00:51,286
a memory limit that is too small, a policy that blocks a port.

12
00:00:51,286 --> 00:00:56,658
Copying the error text into a search engine finds other people's problems.

13
00:00:56,658 --> 00:01:02,321
Following the same questions every time finds yours, usually in a few minutes.

14
00:01:04,067 --> 00:01:06,486
And how does it actually work?

15
00:01:07,033 --> 00:01:10,288
The troubleshooting mindset, in order:

16
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:12,182
1.

17
00:01:12,182 --> 00:01:15,083
Create the crashing Pod and look at it:

18
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:15,788
2.

19
00:01:15,788 --> 00:01:16,331
Events.

20
00:01:16,331 --> 00:01:23,003
Every namespace keeps a short history of what happened to its objects (about an hour).

21
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:23,722
2.

22
00:01:23,722 --> 00:01:24,265
Events.

23
00:01:24,265 --> 00:01:30,936
Every namespace keeps a short history of what happened to its objects (about an hour).

24
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:38,364
Only the warnings, across all namespaces, is a good habit when you do not know where to look yet:

25
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:43,009
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

26
00:01:43,567 --> 00:01:46,866
The exit code tells you how a container ended:

27
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:50,279
Let me try something.

28
00:01:50,279 --> 00:01:53,077
The backend container has no shell:

29
00:01:53,077 --> 00:01:57,794
its image is "distroless" (only the program, nothing else).

30
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:00,002
What should happen?

31
00:02:00,002 --> 00:02:01,319
A process list.

32
00:02:01,319 --> 00:02:02,548
What happened?

33
00:02:02,548 --> 00:02:02,988
exec:

34
00:02:02,988 --> 00:02:03,427
"sh":

35
00:02:03,427 --> 00:02:05,710
executable file not found:

36
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:10,628
kubectl reached the container, but there is no sh in it.

37
00:02:10,628 --> 00:02:16,160
kubectl exec can only run programs that exist inside the image,

38
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:23,097
and minimal images (distroless, scratch) have no shell, ps or wget, on purpose:

39
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:26,434
fewer programs, fewer vulnerabilities.

40
00:02:26,434 --> 00:02:29,508
Which programs does the image have?

41
00:02:31,267 --> 00:02:37,415
kubectl debug adds an ephemeral container to the running Pod, from any image you choose.

42
00:02:37,415 --> 00:02:42,026
With --target, it shares the target container's process namespace,

43
00:02:42,026 --> 00:02:46,776
so it sees the backend's processes, and it shares the Pod's network:

44
00:02:48,533 --> 00:02:49,247
Got it.

45
00:02:49,247 --> 00:02:51,998
What do I need to remember?

46
00:02:52,533 --> 00:02:53,072
Method:

47
00:02:53,072 --> 00:03:01,303
what is broken → expected vs actual → which object → describe/events → logs → configuration → fix → verify.

48
00:03:01,867 --> 00:03:10,721
Events (kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp, describe) explain most problems at once.

49
00:03:11,267 --> 00:03:19,331
kubectl logs --previous for crashes, -c for multi-container Pods; exit codes tell how a container ended.

50
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:23,571
I keep hearing about Helm.

51
00:03:23,571 --> 00:03:25,401
What is it, really?

52
00:03:25,967 --> 00:03:28,946
Helm is the package manager of Kubernetes.

53
00:03:28,946 --> 00:03:41,431
A chart is a folder of YAML templates plus a file of default settings (values.yaml); Helm fills the templates with the values and applies the result to the cluster as one unit,

54
00:03:41,431 --> 00:03:42,141
a release.

55
00:03:42,141 --> 00:03:48,738
Every change to a release is numbered (a revision), so you can see the history and roll back.

56
00:03:48,738 --> 00:03:55,974
If raw YAML files are a recipe written out for exactly four people, a chart is the recipe with blanks:

57
00:03:55,974 --> 00:03:56,471
"flour:

58
00:03:56,471 --> 00:03:58,031
{{ guests × 100 }} g".

59
00:03:58,567 --> 00:04:02,191
By now the course application is a dozen YAML files:

60
00:04:02,191 --> 00:04:05,816
Deployments, Services, a ConfigMap, a Secret, a PVC.

61
00:04:05,816 --> 00:04:12,229
Running it in dev and test means copying all of them and changing a few values in each copy,

62
00:04:12,229 --> 00:04:15,017
then keeping the copies in sync forever.

63
00:04:15,017 --> 00:04:20,733
Helm replaces the copies with one chart and one small values file per environment,

64
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:27,006
installs and upgrades everything in one command, and remembers each version for rollbacks.

65
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:31,186
And how does it actually work?

66
00:04:31,733 --> 00:04:35,529
| Word | Meaning | |---|---| | chart | the package:

67
00:04:35,529 --> 00:04:43,045
templates + default values + metadata | | values | the settings that fill the templates (values.yaml,

68
00:04:43,045 --> 00:04:48,105
-f file, --set key=value) | | release | one installation of a chart,

69
00:04:48,105 --> 00:04:53,686
with a name (shop), in a namespace | | revision | one version of a release:

70
00:04:53,686 --> 00:04:57,333
install = 1, each upgrade or rollback adds one |.

71
00:04:59,067 --> 00:05:06,281
The course chart, helm/learning-app, follows the same layout with templates for the course application.

72
00:05:06,281 --> 00:05:06,421
3.

73
00:05:06,421 --> 00:05:10,273
Check and preview the chart before installing anything:

74
00:05:10,273 --> 00:05:14,685
lint finds mistakes, template prints the YAML Helm would apply:

75
00:05:14,685 --> 00:05:14,825
4.

76
00:05:14,825 --> 00:05:16,506
Install the dev release:

77
00:05:17,067 --> 00:05:17,260
4.

78
00:05:17,260 --> 00:05:19,576
Install the dev release:

79
00:05:20,133 --> 00:05:20,340
5.

80
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:22,924
Upgrade to backend 2.0.0.

81
00:05:22,924 --> 00:05:27,678
--set overrides one value on top of the files:

82
00:05:28,233 --> 00:05:28,446
6.

83
00:05:28,446 --> 00:05:30,678
History and rollback:

84
00:05:32,433 --> 00:05:35,343
How do I see what Kubernetes did?

85
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:38,131
What Helm knows about a release:

86
00:05:38,131 --> 00:05:42,174
the values it was installed with, and the YAML it applied:

87
00:05:43,933 --> 00:05:45,491
Let me try something.

88
00:05:45,491 --> 00:05:50,833
Release a backend version that does not exist, and let Helm wait for it:

89
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:56,125
What should happen?

90
00:05:56,125 --> 00:06:01,384
Revision 4, deployed.

91
00:06:01,384 --> 00:06:04,890
What happened?

92
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:08,646
UPGRADE FAILED.

93
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:17,490
Roll back to the last good revision; the fix of the release itself (building 9.9.9,

94
00:06:17,490 --> 00:06:20,309
or using a real tag) comes after:

95
00:06:22,067 --> 00:06:22,780
Got it.

96
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:25,531
What do I need to remember?

97
00:06:26,067 --> 00:06:34,301
A chart = templates + default values; a release = one installation; every change is a numbered revision.

98
00:06:34,867 --> 00:06:43,346
helm install / upgrade / rollback / uninstall / list / history; lint and template before installing.

99
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:47,871
Values files per environment replace copies of YAML:

100
00:06:47,871 --> 00:06:51,459
dev, test and prod differ only in their values.
